// A Read is permitted if there was formerly a Login whose session id is the
// literal "sess-1". The author WRITES the pinned leaf themselves, as a
// concrete literal (`__drupe.session_id: "sess-1"`). On its own this
// author constraint matches the past Login in BOTH traces (its session is
// "sess-1" in both), so without the pin both traces would permit.
//
// The schema PINS the same leaf to `context.__drupe.session_id` (see
// event.dwschema). Because the pin is injected unconditionally, the predicate
// carries `__drupe.session_id` twice: the author's literal "sess-1" AND
// the pin's `context.__drupe.session_id` (the current Read's session).
// Both must hold. This is the "disagree -> unsatisfiable" contract:
//   trace_1: current Read's session is "sess-1" → pin agrees with the literal
//            → past Login matches → permitted.
//   trace_2: current Read's session is "sess-OTHER" → the pin requires the
//            past Login's session to equal "sess-OTHER", but the literal
//            requires "sess-1"; the two disagree, the predicate is
//            unsatisfiable → NOT permitted.
// The author-written literal cannot relax the pin; it can only add to it.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user, __drupe.session_id: "sess-1" }
};
